WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.769
Welcome back to "late night."
We're here with Jordan Klepper.

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:05.304
You went to Hungary,
and Hungary has been

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:08.041
turning up in the discourse here
a bit more,

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:09.542
certainly amongst conservatives.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.043
What is the draw of Hungary?

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:15.615
Why do we see Tucker Carlson
go there and speak from Hungary?

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.149
-That was the question
we were trying to answer

00:00:17.149 --> 00:00:19.952
when we went there.
I think, quite honestly,

00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:21.454
they have a leader,
Viktor Orban,

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:23.756
who has been called
a "more competent" Trump.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:26.792
Steve Bannon called him
"Trump before Trump."

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:28.160
And he's sort of created

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.663
this conservative wonderland
over there.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:31.998
He's been in power for 12 years,

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.966
something I think
Trump would love to do.

00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:35.235
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:37.237
-He's used wedge issues
to get people on board.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:40.906
Right now
the LGBTQ issues are something

00:00:40.906 --> 00:00:42.975
he's codified
in the constitution,

00:00:42.975 --> 00:00:45.177
taking away rights
from that community.

00:00:45.177 --> 00:00:48.047
He's starting to equate
homosexuality to pedophilia.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:50.016
He's creating sort of a scare
over there

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:52.084
that's getting people
out to the polls.

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:53.986
And what you start
to see is, like,

00:00:53.986 --> 00:00:56.889
nationalism is starting to reign
in Hungary,

00:00:56.889 --> 00:00:58.958
and I think conservatives
in America look over there

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:02.595
and they see somebody
who has the power they want,

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.298
is perhaps a smarter version
of the man they had in charge,

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:08.534
and is able to put people
in power in that country

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:11.203
so that it's much harder
for them to get out of power.

00:01:11.203 --> 00:01:14.206
So it sort of looks like
what I think conservatives

00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:16.709
might think America
could look like in a few years.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.612
-And are there similarities
between what you see there --

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.915
It certainly seemed, maybe
a little bit more in the clip,

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:25.051
a little bit more polite,
a little bit more controlled?

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:27.786
But are there similarities
with a MAGA rally?

00:01:27.786 --> 00:01:29.722
-Some, yes. I mean,
I went to the merch table.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.024
And sort of, like, if you don't
have a Second Amendment,

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:33.192
what do you put
on your T-shirts?

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.594
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.163
-Calvin can't piss on CNN
over here, so what --

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.898
what is the political
discourse, guys?

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.667
-Yeah.
-There's definite similarities.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:43.369
You know, the rhetoric --

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.371
Quite honestly,
they go to Soros as a bad guy,

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.040
who is from Hungary.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.243
And, in fact, some of these
tactics were stolen by America

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.079
to make the boogeyman
that Hungary already had.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:57.016
I was shocked at how many people
referenced Joe Biden

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.384
and specifically talked about

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:01.253
Hunter Biden's laptop
in Budapest.

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:02.688
Like, I'm going over there

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:04.656
talking about
the war in Ukraine,

00:02:04.656 --> 00:02:06.926
which is literally
next to Hungary.

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.427
Which -- I know geography.

00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:10.463
I've known where Hungary is
for literally months now.

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:13.132
That's where it is.
-Yeah.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:15.768
-So they have a war next door,

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:19.405
but still
this obfuscation of reality

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:21.707
and the focus on things
like Hunter Biden's laptop,

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:23.743
that's out there
in the streets of Budapest,

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.978
and people are ready
to engage with me about it.

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:28.981
-So, you're doing a podcast
right now with John Kasich.

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:30.516
John Kasich,
former governor of Ohio.

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:31.684
-It's what the people
asked for.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:32.985
-That's what they asked for.

00:02:32.985 --> 00:02:34.487
And he's been on this show,
and I always thought,

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:35.788
"You know who you'd love?
Klepper."

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.489
-"You guys got to do
a thing together!"

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:38.791
-So, how did this happen?

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.993
Because this does not seem
like a natural pairing.

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:43.129
-You know, it's a governor
meet cute that I think --

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.232
Things got weird
during the pandemic.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.733
-Sure.
-You know, we were all looking

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.269
for connection in strange ways.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:52.372
We all dabbled
with our own governors.

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:54.340
We know. We went through this.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.009
-It's not weird.
It's natural, you know?

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:58.444
So you find a governor
in a midsize state,

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.112
and you start to meet over Zoom.

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:02.315
And some folks
actually said, like,

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:04.249
"Hey, Governor Kasich
is looking to do a podcast

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:06.085
and talk to somebody
with a different point of view.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:08.254
I think you guys
would hit it off."

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:11.023
And I wasn't super into
doing a political podcast.

00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:14.560
And as soon as we got on a Zoom
together, I was shocked

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.662
because we talked about
Rage Against the Machine

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:17.930
for most of the time.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.633
-Yeah.
-He's a huge music fan.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:22.635
And literally we talked music,
we talked sports,

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:25.004
we talked everything
away from politics.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:28.007
So by the time we actually did
get to some political issues,

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.275
we actually liked each other

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:32.011
and we could kind of
meet closer to empathy,

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.814
as opposed to just, like,
fisticuffs over the economy.

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:36.215
And we kind of thought, like,

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:39.118
maybe this is sort of
a unique way to approach

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:41.720
these "bipartisan"
conversations.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.223
Like, start with culture,
start with things you love,

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:46.525
and then get to politics
in kind of an organic way.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.160
-So, I mean,
I think that's great.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.829
And I'm always so excited
when people from, you know,

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:52.431
differing political views talk.

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:55.835
But, of course, Governor Kasich
is an anti-Trump Republican.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:58.771
Is there any --
Do you -- Does he feel like

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.339
there's any future of the party

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:02.908
for the lane
that he currently lives in?

00:04:02.908 --> 00:04:06.545
-I mean, Governor Kasich
often talks about

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:08.748
feeling that the
Republican Party left him,

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.249
he didn't leave
the Republican Party.

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:11.917
And he's much more
of an optimist than I am

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.719
when we talk about the future.

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.589
I don't feel super great
about it.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.257
I don't want to put words
in his mouth,

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:20.626
but I do think he is optimistic

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:22.661
that there are a lot
of moderates in this country

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:24.030
who are looking for people

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.032
to voice the concerns
that they have.

00:04:26.032 --> 00:04:27.833
Actually I don't disagree
with him there.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:29.501
But when I go to places
like CPAC,

00:04:29.501 --> 00:04:30.937
what I'm seeing is --

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:34.307
I don't see those voices
being elevated.

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:36.008
I went to CPAC,
and I watched people

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:38.911
like Marjorie Taylor Greene
walk through there like Beyoncé.

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.913
And so she's got 10 people
behind her

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.682
with cameras taking pictures.

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:44.383
And there's a lot
of young conservatives

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:45.851
who are looking for attention,

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:47.787
and they see Boebert
and they see MTG,

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:49.922
and they're like, "That's how
you get attention nowadays."

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:52.491
And so, for me, I see the future
of the Republican Party,

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:53.826
the only lane seems to be

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:56.395
on the farther right
of Donald Trump.

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:58.297
I think Governor Kasich
might think it's somewhere else,

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:01.334
but I don't see any evidence
of it heading that way.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.436
-You were at the insurrection,
January 6th.

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:08.207
You went back.
Are you surprised?

00:05:08.207 --> 00:05:09.675
-As a member
of "The Daily Show."

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.343
-Yeah.
-It's not like I left something

00:05:11.343 --> 00:05:13.346
inside the Capitol like,
"My wallet was in here.

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:15.348
It was in this lectern
when I flipped it over."

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:17.149
-You went back to try
to put back a laptop

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:18.618
you'd stolen
before you got caught?

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:19.952
-Yeah.

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:21.753
Legally,
I can't get too into it,

00:05:21.753 --> 00:05:24.324
but I did go back to D.C., yes.

00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:25.958
-Watching it --
I was watching it on TV.

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:27.593
I can't imagine
what it was like for you.

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.362
You thought like,
"Oh, this changes everything,"

00:05:29.362 --> 00:05:31.297
and I don't know if it has.

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:32.798
-I don't think it has.
-Yeah.

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:35.101
-I think it did feel like a day
that could change everything.

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:37.203
I think we keep
having those days of,

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:38.638
"This is as far as we can go.

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:40.706
We all have to
get on the same page.

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:43.208
There's a common enemy,
and it's chaos.

00:05:43.208 --> 00:05:46.112
If we could all rally around
this flag on this day."

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:47.713
And it felt like that lasted

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:49.882
for an hour or so
after the insurrection.

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:51.284
-Yeah. It was a nice hour.
-It was a good hour.

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:53.719
-It was a good hour.
-It was a real nice hour.

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:55.053
I don't know.

00:05:55.053 --> 00:05:57.490
I don't want to say I'm shocked,
because I'm not.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:00.426
I think, like,
the news cycle moves so quick,

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:02.929
we don't have to have a memory
of those things.

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:04.730
And when I go
and talk to people --

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.099
I wouldn't talk to people
months after the insurrection.

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:09.401
And I was shocked how quick
that narrative changed.

00:06:09.401 --> 00:06:11.037
And when I went back
a year later,

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:12.605
Matt Gaetz,
Marjorie Taylor Greene,

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:14.239
they're out there talking
about other narratives

00:06:14.239 --> 00:06:16.209
that they want to push,
and so, sadly, I don't think

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:18.043
we remember the important things
from that day,

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:19.479
and that could bite us
in the ass.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:21.113
-Well, thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:22.748
Thanks for getting out.
It's just fantastic.

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:24.516
Every time I see that
you've gone somewhere new,

00:06:24.516 --> 00:06:25.751
I get very excited to watch it.

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:27.019
I really appreciate
you making time.

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:28.754
You guys, that's Jordan Klepper.
-Thank you.

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:30.389
-You can subscribe
to "Kasich &amp; Klepper"

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:32.124
wherever you listen to podcasts.

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:33.692
And "Jordan Klepper
Fingers the Globe:

00:06:33.692 --> 00:06:34.960
Hungary for Democracy"

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.397
premieres April 21st
on Comedy Central.

